-- Q1: medium / L2 — Section 382 COBE requirement
(6, 'Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns', $EXPL$Whitaker Corp undergoes an ownership change under Section 382. Within 18 months of the change date, the acquiring shareholders shut down Whitaker's original manufacturing operations and redirect all assets to a new line of business. How does this affect Whitaker's Section 382 annual limitation on pre-change NOLs?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The Section 382 limitation is reduced to zero because the continuity of business enterprise requirement is not met", "The Section 382 limitation continues as calculated using the stock value and long-term tax-exempt rate", "The Section 382 limitation is reduced by 50% for the first two years following the ownership change", "The pre-change NOLs are permanently disallowed but the Section 382 limitation applies to future losses"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 382(c), if the loss corporation does not continue its business enterprise for at least two years after an ownership change, the Section 382 annual limitation is reduced to zero. This effectively eliminates the ability to use pre-change NOLs entirely. Wrong (B): The limitation does not continue unaffected; discontinuing the business triggers the zero-limitation rule. Wrong (C): There is no 50% reduction provision — the limitation drops to zero, not halfway. Wrong (D): The pre-change NOLs are not permanently disallowed by statute; rather the annual limitation becomes zero, preventing any utilization.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Intercompany dividend elimination
(6, 'Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns', $EXPL$Fulton Corp owns 100% of Garner Inc., and both are members of a consolidated return group. During 2025, Garner pays a $200,000 dividend to Fulton. How is this dividend treated on the group's consolidated return?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The dividend is included in Fulton's income but offset by a 100% dividends-received deduction", "The dividend is eliminated entirely in consolidation and has no effect on consolidated taxable income", "The dividend is included in consolidated taxable income at a reduced rate of 50%", "The dividend is deferred until Garner leaves the consolidated group"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.1502-13(f), intercompany dividends between members of a consolidated group are eliminated in computing consolidated taxable income. No dividends-received deduction analysis is needed because the item is simply removed during consolidation. Wrong (A): The 100% DRD applies to separate return filers with 80%+ ownership, but in consolidation the dividend is eliminated outright, not deducted. Wrong (C): There is no reduced inclusion rate for intercompany dividends — they are fully eliminated. Wrong (D): Intercompany dividends are eliminated currently, not deferred.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Section 384 limitation on pre-acquisition losses vs built-in gains
(6, 'Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns', $EXPL$Mercer Group acquires 100% of Talbot Corp in a taxable stock purchase. Talbot has assets with a fair market value of $2,000,000 and a tax basis of $1,200,000, creating an $800,000 net unrealized built-in gain. Mercer has $500,000 of pre-acquisition NOL carryforwards. During the first year after the acquisition, Talbot sells appreciated assets and recognizes $300,000 of built-in gain. Can Mercer use its pre-acquisition NOLs to offset Talbot's recognized built-in gain?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Yes, because the NOLs and built-in gains are combined on the consolidated return without limitation", "Yes, but only to the extent of the Section 382 annual limitation", "No, Section 384 prevents pre-acquisition losses from offsetting recognized built-in gains of an acquired corporation during the 5-year recognition period", "No, the built-in gains must first be offset by Talbot's own pre-acquisition NOLs before the group can apply any losses"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): IRC Section 384 prevents a corporation from using its pre-acquisition NOLs to offset the recognized built-in gains of another corporation that joins the group, during the 5-year recognition period following the acquisition. This prevents tax-motivated acquisitions designed to shelter built-in gains with unrelated losses. Wrong (A): Consolidation does not eliminate the Section 384 limitation — pre-acquisition losses face restrictions. Wrong (B): Section 382 limits usage of the loss corporation's NOLs, but Section 384 specifically addresses the cross-use of losses against another corporation's built-in gains. Wrong (D): There is no requirement that Talbot's own NOLs offset its gains first; the rule restricts Mercer's pre-acquisition losses.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: medium / L2 — Section 382 anti-stuffing capital contributions
(6, 'Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns', $EXPL$Davenport Corp has $4,000,000 of NOL carryforwards. Six months before an anticipated ownership change, Davenport's shareholders contribute $1,500,000 of additional capital to increase the company's stock value and raise the Section 382 annual limitation. The long-term tax-exempt rate is 4%. How does Section 382(l)(1) affect the computation of the annual limitation?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The additional capital is included in the stock value, increasing the annual limitation to $220,000", "The additional capital is included only if contributed more than one year before the ownership change", "The annual limitation is computed using only the stock value at the time of the ownership change with no adjustment", "The $1,500,000 capital contribution is excluded from the stock value because it was contributed within two years of the ownership change as part of a plan to increase the limitation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): IRC Section 382(l)(1) provides an anti-stuffing rule that excludes capital contributions made within two years before an ownership change if they were part of a plan to increase the Section 382 limitation. The stock value used in the limitation calculation is reduced by the tainted contribution. Wrong (A): Including the full contribution would circumvent the anti-abuse purpose of Section 382(l)(1). Wrong (B): The statute uses a two-year lookback period, not one year. Wrong (C): The value is not simply the stock value at the change date — it must be adjusted for tainted capital contributions.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — SRLY cumulative register
(6, 'Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns', $EXPL$Hawkins Inc. had a $400,000 NOL before joining the Langford consolidated group. In Year 1 as a member, Hawkins contributes $120,000 of separate company taxable income. In Year 2, Hawkins has a $30,000 loss. In Year 3, Hawkins contributes $90,000 of separate company taxable income. What is the cumulative amount of Hawkins's pre-acquisition NOL that the group may use through Year 3?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$180,000, based on the cumulative net positive income Hawkins contributed over three years", "$210,000, because the Year 2 loss does not reduce the cumulative register", "$120,000, because only Year 1 income counts and each year resets the limitation", "$90,000, because only the most recent year's contribution is considered"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the SRLY rules in Treas. Reg. Section 1.1502-21(c), the limitation on pre-acquisition NOLs is based on the member's cumulative contribution to consolidated taxable income across all years since joining. The cumulative register is $120,000 minus $30,000 plus $90,000 = $180,000. Wrong (B): The Year 2 loss does reduce the cumulative register — the SRLY limitation tracks net cumulative income. Wrong (C): The SRLY limitation is cumulative across all years, not reset annually. Wrong (D): Only using the most recent year ignores the cumulative nature of the register.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: easy / L1 — Consolidated return filing mechanism
(6, 'Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns', $EXPL$How does an affiliated group of corporations elect to file a consolidated federal income tax return?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["By submitting a separate election form to the IRS at least 90 days before the due date", "By filing a single Form 1120 as a consolidated return with all members included", "By having each member file a separate Form 1120 and attaching a consolidation schedule", "By filing Form 1065 with a consolidated election box checked"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.1502-75(a), the consolidated return election is made simply by filing a single Form 1120 as a consolidated return that includes all members of the affiliated group. No separate election form is required. Wrong (A): There is no separate pre-filing election form required — the act of filing the consolidated return constitutes the election. Wrong (C): Members do not file separate returns with a consolidation schedule; the group files a single return. Wrong (D): Form 1065 is for partnerships; affiliated corporations use Form 1120.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q7: hard / L4 — Evaluating Section 382 COBE + NUBIG interaction
(6, 'Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns', $EXPL$Cromwell Holdings acquires 100% of Radcliffe Corp, which has $6,000,000 of pre-change NOLs, a stock value of $3,000,000, and a net unrealized built-in gain (NUBIG) of $1,200,000. The long-term tax-exempt rate is 4%, yielding an annual Section 382 limitation of $120,000. In Year 1 post-change, Radcliffe recognizes $500,000 of built-in gains from appreciated asset sales. In Year 2, Cromwell plans to discontinue Radcliffe's historic business entirely. Evaluate the interaction of the NUBIG adjustment and the continuity of business enterprise requirement on Radcliffe's NOL utilization.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The NUBIG increases the Year 1 limitation to $620,000, and Year 2 limitation remains $120,000 because COBE only applies at the initial change date", "The NUBIG has no effect because the COBE failure retroactively disallows all NOL usage from Year 1 forward", "The NUBIG increases the Year 1 limitation to $620,000, and the Year 2 limitation drops to zero because failing COBE within two years eliminates the Section 382 limitation prospectively", "The COBE failure prevents any NUBIG adjustment in Year 1, limiting NOL usage to $120,000 in both years"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 382(h)(1)(A), recognized built-in gain in Year 1 increases the Section 382 limitation for that year by $500,000 (to $620,000). However, discontinuing Radcliffe's business in Year 2 violates the two-year COBE requirement under Section 382(c), reducing the Section 382 limitation to zero prospectively. Year 1 deductions already taken are not retroactively disallowed. Wrong (A): The COBE requirement is ongoing for two years — failing it after Year 1 does reduce the limitation to zero going forward. Wrong (B): The COBE failure operates prospectively, not retroactively; Year 1 deductions are not clawed back. Wrong (D): The COBE failure in Year 2 does not retroactively affect the Year 1 NUBIG adjustment.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Common parent definition
(6, 'Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns', $EXPL$What role does the "common parent" serve in a consolidated return group under Section 1504?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["It guarantees payment of all subsidiaries' separate tax liabilities", "It provides management services to all group members as a regulatory requirement", "It files the consolidated return but has no liability for the group's tax", "It is the corporation that directly owns at least 80% of the vote and value of at least one includible corporation and acts as the group's agent for tax matters"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 1504(a)(1), the common parent is the corporation at the top of the affiliated group that directly owns at least 80% of the voting power and value of at least one includible corporation. Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.1502-77, it serves as the sole agent for the group in dealings with the IRS. Wrong (A): While all members share joint and several liability, the parent's specific role is as the group's agent and filer, not merely a guarantor. Wrong (B): Providing management services is a business function, not a tax law requirement of the common parent. Wrong (C): The parent does file the return but also bears joint and several liability for the group's tax.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Intercompany interest elimination
(6, 'Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns', $EXPL$In a consolidated group, Parent Corp lends $500,000 to Subsidiary Corp at a market interest rate of 5%. During 2025, Subsidiary pays $25,000 of interest to Parent. How are the interest income and interest expense treated on the consolidated return?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Parent's $25,000 of interest income and Subsidiary's $25,000 of interest expense are both eliminated in consolidation", "Parent reports $25,000 of interest income and Subsidiary deducts $25,000 of interest expense separately on the consolidated return", "Only the interest expense is deductible; the interest income is excluded from the consolidated return", "The interest payments are reclassified as dividends because they occur between related parties"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.1502-13, intercompany transactions between consolidated group members are eliminated in computing consolidated taxable income. Parent's interest income and Subsidiary's interest expense offset and produce no net effect on the consolidated return. Wrong (B): Separate reporting of matching items applies to separate return filers, not members of a consolidated group. Wrong (C): Both the income and expense sides are eliminated, not just one. Wrong (D): Intercompany interest is not reclassified as a dividend; it is simply eliminated in consolidation.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: hard / L4 — Evaluating Section 384 + Section 382 overlap
(6, 'Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns', $EXPL$Bellamy Group has $2,000,000 of pre-acquisition NOLs and acquires Stratton Corp, which has assets with an FMV of $5,000,000 and a tax basis of $3,000,000 (NUBIG of $2,000,000). The acquisition triggers an ownership change for Bellamy under Section 382, with an annual limitation of $200,000. In Year 1, Stratton recognizes $800,000 of built-in gains. Bellamy's tax advisor must determine whether Bellamy's pre-acquisition NOLs can offset Stratton's recognized built-in gains. Evaluate the interaction of Section 382 and Section 384.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Section 382 limits Bellamy to $200,000 of NOL usage, but Section 384 does not apply because the gains are Stratton's, not Bellamy's", "Section 384 prevents Bellamy's pre-acquisition NOLs from offsetting Stratton's $800,000 of recognized built-in gains, and Section 382 independently limits Bellamy's total NOL usage to $200,000 against other income", "Both sections merge into a single $200,000 limitation that applies against all income including built-in gains", "Section 384 overrides Section 382 entirely, allowing unlimited NOL usage against non-built-in-gain income"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 384 specifically prevents a corporation from using its pre-acquisition NOLs to offset recognized built-in gains of another corporation acquired in the same transaction during the 5-year recognition period. Separately, Section 382 limits how much of Bellamy's NOLs can be used annually against other income. These are independent limitations addressing different policy concerns. Wrong (A): Section 384 applies precisely because the gains belong to the acquired corporation and the losses belong to the acquirer. Wrong (C): The two limitations are independent and do not merge into a single dollar cap. Wrong (D): Section 384 does not override Section 382; both apply in their respective scopes.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Section 382 recognition period
(6, 'Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns', $EXPL$For purposes of Section 382, how long is the recognition period for built-in gains and losses following an ownership change?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["3 years from the date of the ownership change", "The remaining NOL carryforward period", "5 years from the date of the ownership change", "10 years from the date of the ownership change"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 382(h)(7), the recognition period for built-in gains and losses is the 5-year period beginning on the date of the ownership change. Gains or losses recognized during this period may increase or limit the Section 382 annual limitation. Wrong (A): The 3-year period is the testing period for determining whether an ownership change occurred, not the recognition period. Wrong (B): The recognition period is a fixed 5-year window, not tied to the NOL carryforward period. Wrong (D): Ten years is not the statutory recognition period under Section 382.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — CNOL apportionment methodology for departing member
(6, 'Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns', $EXPL$The Ellington consolidated group has a $600,000 CNOL from 2024. Member X contributed $400,000 of the losses and Member Y contributed $200,000. At the end of 2025, Ellington sells all of Member Y's stock to an unrelated buyer. How much of the 2024 CNOL does Member Y take upon leaving the group?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$600,000, because any departing member may take the entire CNOL", "$400,000, based on Member X's contribution which transfers to the departing member", "$0, because CNOLs remain with the consolidated group and departing members forfeit their share", "$200,000, based on Member Y's proportional contribution to the CNOL in the year it was generated"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.1502-21(b)(2), when a member departs a consolidated group, it takes the portion of any CNOL attributable to it based on the member's contribution to the consolidated loss in the year the CNOL arose. Member Y contributed $200,000 of the $600,000 loss, so it takes $200,000. Wrong (A): The entire CNOL does not transfer — only the departing member's proportional share. Wrong (B): Member X's contribution stays with the group, not with the departing member. Wrong (C): Departing members do take their proportional share of CNOLs; they do not forfeit them.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: easy / L2 — Separate return limitation year definition
(6, 'Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns', $EXPL$Henley Corp generates a $250,000 NOL in 2023 while filing its own separate federal income tax return. In 2024, Henley joins the Preston consolidated group. For SRLY purposes, how is Henley's 2023 tax year classified?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A separate return limitation year, because Henley was not a member of the Preston group when the NOL arose", "A consolidated net operating loss year, because joining a group converts all prior NOLs to CNOLs", "An unrestricted loss year, because the NOL predates the consolidated election", "A pre-acquisition loss year exempt from any limitation because it was generated independently"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.1502-1(f), a separate return limitation year is any tax year in which a member filed a separate return or was a member of a different consolidated group. Henley's 2023 year qualifies as a SRLY, subjecting the $250,000 NOL to the cumulative income limitation. Wrong (B): Joining a group does not convert prior separate-year NOLs into consolidated NOLs — they retain SRLY status. Wrong (C): There is no unrestricted loss year category — pre-acquisition separate-year losses face SRLY restrictions. Wrong (D): There is no blanket exemption for independently generated losses.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — Investment adjustment ordering
(6, 'Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns', $EXPL$In what order are investment adjustments applied to a parent's stock basis in a consolidated subsidiary under Treas. Reg. Section 1.1502-32?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Distributions first, then losses, then income items", "Income and gain items first, then losses and deductions, then distributions", "Losses and deductions first, then income items, then distributions", "All items are applied simultaneously at year-end with no prescribed order"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.1502-32(b), positive adjustments (taxable income, tax-exempt income) are applied first, followed by negative adjustments (losses, deductions, nondeductible expenses), and then distributions. This ordering prevents artificial creation of excess loss accounts. Wrong (A): Distributions are applied last, not first, to ensure income adjustments are reflected before reducing basis for distributions. Wrong (C): Losses are applied after income items, not before. Wrong (D): The regulation prescribes a specific ordering; simultaneous application is incorrect.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: hard / L4 — Loss disallowance under Reg 1.1502-36
(6, 'Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns', $EXPL$Ashford Group owns 100% of Benton Corp with a stock basis of $800,000. Over three years, Benton generates $200,000 of income and $500,000 of losses allocated to Ashford, resulting in an adjusted stock basis of $500,000. Benton also has $300,000 of unrealized losses in its assets (FMV $400,000, basis $700,000). Ashford sells all Benton stock to an unrelated buyer for $400,000, recognizing a $100,000 stock loss. Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.1502-36, evaluate the treatment of this stock loss.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The $100,000 stock loss is fully deductible because it reflects a genuine economic decline in Benton's value", "The stock loss is recharacterized as a capital contribution to Benton and added back to Ashford's basis", "The stock loss is subject to basis reduction under Reg. Section 1.1502-36, which may reduce or eliminate the loss to prevent duplicating Benton's unrealized asset losses at both the stock and asset level", "The stock loss is disallowed entirely and Ashford must instead reduce its basis in other group members' stock"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Treas. Reg. Section 1.1502-36 addresses loss duplication when subsidiary stock is transferred outside the group. Because Benton has $300,000 of net unrealized asset losses, the regulation requires Ashford to reduce its stock basis before recognizing any loss, preventing the same economic loss from being deducted at both the stock and asset levels. Wrong (A): Even if the value decline is genuine, Reg. 1.1502-36 prevents duplication of losses embedded in the subsidiary's assets. Wrong (B): Recharacterization as a capital contribution is not the mechanism used by Reg. 1.1502-36. Wrong (D): The basis reduction applies to the specific subsidiary's stock being sold, not to other group members' stock.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q16: hard / L4 — Intercompany obligation satisfaction with gain/loss
(6, 'Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns', $EXPL$In a consolidated group, Parent Corp lends $1,000,000 to Subsidiary at par. Two years later, when market interest rates have risen and the note's fair market value has declined to $850,000, Parent sells the intercompany note to an unrelated third party for $850,000. Subsidiary's obligation to the third party remains at $1,000,000 face value. Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.1502-13(g), evaluate the tax consequences of this transaction.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Parent recognizes a $150,000 loss and Subsidiary has no income because the obligation still exists at face value", "Both the $150,000 loss and any corresponding income are permanently eliminated in consolidation", "Parent recognizes only $75,000 of loss, with the remaining $75,000 deferred until Subsidiary repays the note", "Under the deemed satisfaction rules, Parent recognizes a $150,000 loss and Subsidiary simultaneously recognizes $150,000 of discharge of indebtedness income, producing no net effect on consolidated taxable income"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.1502-13(g)(3), when an intercompany obligation ceases to be an intercompany obligation (here, because an outsider acquired it), the debtor and creditor are treated as if the obligation were satisfied. Parent recognizes a $150,000 loss and Subsidiary recognizes $150,000 of COD income, netting to zero on the consolidated return. Wrong (A): The deemed satisfaction rules require Subsidiary to recognize income even though the obligation technically continues. Wrong (B): The items are not permanently eliminated — they are matched under the single-entity principles. Wrong (C): There is no partial deferral mechanism; the full deemed satisfaction occurs when the obligation leaves the group.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q17: hard / L3 — Section 382 equity structure shift via reorganization
(6, 'Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns', $EXPL$Rutherford Corp has $3,000,000 of NOL carryforwards. Rutherford merges into Caldwell Inc. in a tax-free Type A reorganization under Section 368(a)(1)(A). As part of the merger, Rutherford's former shareholders receive 30% of Caldwell's outstanding stock, and Caldwell's original shareholders retain 70%. The fair market value of Rutherford's stock before the merger was $2,000,000 and the long-term tax-exempt rate is 4%. How does Section 382 apply to Rutherford's pre-change NOLs after the merger?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The merger constitutes an equity structure shift that is an ownership change under Section 382, limiting annual NOL usage to $80,000", "Section 382 does not apply because tax-free reorganizations are exempt from ownership change testing", "The NOLs are fully available without limitation because Rutherford's shareholders received stock in the reorganization", "The annual limitation is $120,000 because the value is measured by Caldwell's post-merger stock price"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 382(g)(3), a tax-free reorganization is an equity structure shift. Because Rutherford's former shareholders went from 100% ownership to only 30%, the 5-percent shareholder analysis shows a greater-than-50-percentage-point increase by Caldwell's shareholders, triggering an ownership change. The annual limitation is $2,000,000 multiplied by 4% = $80,000. Wrong (B): Tax-free reorganizations are explicitly included as equity structure shifts subject to Section 382 testing. Wrong (C): Receipt of stock does not exempt the transaction from Section 382 limitations. Wrong (D): The limitation uses the loss corporation's pre-change stock value, not the acquiring corporation's value.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — Recognized built-in loss (RBIL) during recognition period
(6, 'Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns', $EXPL$Pemberton Corp undergoes an ownership change under Section 382 with an annual limitation of $150,000. Pemberton has a net unrealized built-in loss (NUBIL) of $600,000 at the change date. In Year 1 post-change, Pemberton sells an asset for $200,000 that had a basis of $350,000 at the change date, recognizing a $150,000 loss. How does the NUBIL affect the treatment of this $150,000 recognized built-in loss?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The recognized built-in loss increases the Section 382 annual limitation by $150,000", "The $150,000 recognized built-in loss is treated as a pre-change loss and is subject to the $150,000 annual Section 382 limitation", "The recognized built-in loss is not subject to Section 382 because it arises from a post-change asset sale", "The NUBIL has no effect on recognized losses; only the NOL carryforward is limited"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 382(h)(1)(B), if a loss corporation has a NUBIL at the ownership change date, any recognized built-in losses during the 5-year recognition period are treated as pre-change losses subject to the Section 382 annual limitation. The $150,000 loss consumes the entire $150,000 annual limit for Year 1. Wrong (A): Recognized built-in losses reduce available limitation — only recognized built-in gains increase it. Wrong (C): The timing of the sale is irrelevant; what matters is that the loss was built in at the change date and recognized during the recognition period. Wrong (D): Section 382 applies to both NOL carryforwards and recognized built-in losses during the recognition period.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Consolidated net capital loss carryback
(6, 'Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns', $EXPL$The Thornton consolidated group generates a $400,000 consolidated net capital loss in 2025. The group had $250,000 of net capital gains in 2023 and $100,000 of net capital gains in 2022. Under the consolidated capital loss rules, how may the group use this capital loss?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The capital loss may only be carried forward 5 years and may not be carried back", "The capital loss is converted to an ordinary loss and deducted against 2025 ordinary income", "The capital loss may be carried back 3 years, offsetting capital gains in 2022 and 2023, with any remaining loss carried forward up to 5 years", "The capital loss is deducted against up to $3,000 of ordinary income, with the remainder carried forward indefinitely"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 1212(a), a corporation's net capital loss may be carried back 3 years and forward 5 years, offsetting net capital gains in those years. The consolidated group can carry back the $400,000 capital loss to 2022 and 2023, absorbing $350,000 ($100,000 + $250,000), and carry the remaining $50,000 forward. Wrong (A): Corporate capital losses may be carried back — the 3-year carryback is available. Wrong (B): Corporate capital losses are not converted to ordinary losses; they retain their capital character. Wrong (D): The $3,000 ordinary income offset applies to individuals, not corporations.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — Excess charitable contribution carryforward
(6, 'Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns', $EXPL$For how many years may a corporation carry forward excess charitable contributions that exceed the annual deduction limit?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["3 years", "10 years", "Indefinitely", "5 years"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 170(d)(2), a corporation may carry forward charitable contributions exceeding the 10% of taxable income limitation for up to 5 years. This applies to both separate and consolidated return filers. Wrong (A): The 3-year period does not apply to corporate charitable contribution carryforwards. Wrong (B): The 10-year period is not the statutory carryforward for excess charitable contributions. Wrong (C): Unlike post-TCJA NOLs, charitable contribution carryforwards are not indefinite — they expire after 5 years.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: medium / L3 — Intercompany like-kind exchange
(6, 'Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns', $EXPL$In a consolidated group, Sub A exchanges a warehouse (basis $400,000, FMV $600,000) with Sub B for an office building (basis $350,000, FMV $600,000) in a transaction qualifying under Section 1031. Two years later, Sub B sells the warehouse to an unrelated party for $650,000. What total gain does the consolidated group recognize in the year of the external sale?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$250,000, reflecting the single-entity result of $650,000 sale price minus Sub A's original $400,000 basis", "$50,000, reflecting only Sub B's gain above the exchanged basis", "$200,000, reflecting the deferred intercompany gain that was excluded under Section 1031", "$0, because the Section 1031 deferral carries through to the external sale permanently"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.1502-13 and Section 1031, Sub A's $200,000 built-in gain is deferred in the intercompany exchange. When Sub B sells externally for $650,000, the matching rule triggers recognition of the deferred gain plus Sub B's additional appreciation, producing the single-entity result of $650,000 minus $400,000 = $250,000. Wrong (B): This ignores the deferred intercompany gain from the original exchange. Wrong (C): The $200,000 was deferred, not permanently excluded — the full single-entity gain of $250,000 is recognized. Wrong (D): Section 1031 deferral does not extend to sales to unrelated parties; the matching rule triggers recognition.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q22: easy / L2 — Indirect ownership through chain of includible corporations
(6, 'Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns', $EXPL$Wellington Corp owns 90% of the stock of Avery Inc., and Avery Inc. owns 85% of the stock of Canton Corp. All three are domestic C corporations. Wellington owns no stock in Canton directly. Is Canton Corp eligible to be included in Wellington's consolidated return group?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No, because Wellington does not directly own any Canton stock", "Yes, because Canton is connected to Wellington through a chain of 80% ownership via Avery", "No, because the combined indirect ownership of 76.5% fails the 80% test", "Yes, but only if Canton separately elects to join the consolidated group"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1504(a)(1), an affiliated group includes each includible corporation connected to the common parent through a chain of 80% vote-and-value ownership. Avery meets the 80% test with Wellington (90%), and Canton meets the 80% test with Avery (85%). Canton is therefore included through the ownership chain. Wrong (A): Direct ownership by the parent is not required — indirect chain ownership satisfies the test. Wrong (C): The 80% test is applied at each link in the chain, not by multiplying percentages; each link individually exceeds 80%. Wrong (D): New members joining an existing consolidated group are automatically included — no separate election is required.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q23: medium / L2 — Section 382 long-term tax-exempt rate role
(6, 'Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns', $EXPL$Winslow Corp undergoes an ownership change under Section 382. The IRS publishes a long-term tax-exempt rate of 4.5% for the month in which the ownership change occurs, and Winslow's stock value immediately before the change is $4,000,000. What does the long-term tax-exempt rate determine in the Section 382 limitation calculation?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The rate at which pre-change NOLs are discounted to present value for deduction purposes", "The maximum percentage of the total pre-change NOL balance that can be used each year", "The annual dollar limitation, computed as the loss corporation's stock value multiplied by the long-term tax-exempt rate, producing a $180,000 annual limit for Winslow", "The interest rate applied to any unused Section 382 limitation carried forward to future years"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 382(b)(1), the Section 382 annual limitation equals the value of the loss corporation's stock immediately before the ownership change multiplied by the long-term tax-exempt rate. For Winslow, this is $4,000,000 multiplied by 4.5% = $180,000 per year. Wrong (A): NOLs are not discounted to present value — the rate sets the annual dollar cap on usage. Wrong (B): The limitation is a dollar amount based on stock value, not a percentage of the NOL balance. Wrong (D): Unused limitation carries forward at full face value with no interest rate applied.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q24: medium / L3 — Section 382(h)(3)(B) de minimis threshold for NUBIG/NUBIL
(6, 'Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns', $EXPL$Oakmont Corp undergoes an ownership change under Section 382. Oakmont's assets have a total fair market value of $50,000,000 and a total tax basis of $47,500,000, producing a net unrealized built-in gain of $2,500,000. The Section 382(h)(3)(B) de minimis threshold is the lesser of 15% of asset FMV or $10,000,000. How does the de minimis rule apply to Oakmont's built-in gain position?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The NUBIG of $2,500,000 increases the Section 382 limitation when recognized because it exceeds the de minimis threshold", "The $2,500,000 NUBIG is reduced by the de minimis amount, leaving only the excess subject to Section 382 adjustments", "The NUBIG is ignored entirely because all built-in gains below $10,000,000 are exempt from Section 382", "The $2,500,000 NUBIG is treated as zero because it falls below the de minimis threshold of $7,500,000 (15% of $50,000,000), meaning no built-in gain adjustments apply"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 382(h)(3)(B), a loss corporation's NUBIG or NUBIL is treated as zero if it does not exceed the lesser of 15% of the fair market value of the corporation's assets (here, 15% of $50,000,000 = $7,500,000) or $10,000,000. Since $2,500,000 is below $7,500,000, the de minimis rule applies and no built-in gain adjustments are made. Wrong (A): The NUBIG does not increase the limitation because the de minimis threshold eliminates it entirely. Wrong (B): The de minimis rule is not a reduction — it treats the entire NUBIG as zero when below the threshold. Wrong (C): The threshold is the lesser of 15% of FMV or $10,000,000, not a flat $10,000,000 — both tests must be checked.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
